WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:09.060
KASLIN FIELDS: Welcome to
Architecting with Google Cloud.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.280
I'm developer advocate,
Kaslin Fields.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.660
And today, we're
going to explore

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.110
the architecture of Apollo with
engineering lead, Adam Zionts.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:16.860
Welcome, Adam.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:17.280
ADAM ZIONTS: Thank you.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:18.780
I'm really happy
to be here, Kaslin.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.910
KASLIN FIELDS: So I
know Apollo provides

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.250
an implementation of GraphQL.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.250
Could you first tell us a little
bit about what GraphQL is?

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:27.540
ADAM ZIONTS: Definitely.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.540
GraphQL is an API
technology that

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:35.340
helps developers provide access
to all of their data via graph

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:35.880
interface.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.380
QL actually stands
for query language.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.520
So it's a query language
to get at the data graph.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.560
And so that differs
from the usual kind

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.710
of hierarchical pattern
that REST provides.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:50.080
It's been hugely adopted by
a lot of major companies,

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.520
including Walmart,
Expedia, Netflix, Airbnb,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.790
on down the line, and
originally came out of Facebook

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:00.732
to help provide an API
to the social graph.

00:01:00.732 --> 00:01:02.440
KASLIN FIELDS: That
makes a lot of sense.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.023
And it sounds like
a lot of businesses

00:01:04.023 --> 00:01:08.570
are finding benefits in
this style of querying.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:09.570
ADAM ZIONTS: Definitely.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:13.200
I think that some of
the biggest benefits

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.740
are just having all of your data
in one place helps to unlock

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:17.710
a lot of things.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.545
First of all, it cuts down
to the amount of redundancy.

00:01:20.545 --> 00:01:22.170
Where, if you have
all that data there,

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.270
you're not going to end up
recreating the same thing over

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:24.840
and over.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.970
And second of all,
it opens up that data

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:30.120
to people who might want to have
access to it who aren't just

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.820
in engineering, people like
product management or sales.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:35.620
You want people to
get into that data,

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.190
understand how it's
being used, who

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:41.010
is looking at it to help
drive the right kinds

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:43.590
of conversations and unlock the
most value for that business.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:44.423
KASLIN FIELDS: Cool.

00:01:44.423 --> 00:01:47.790
So that's a little bit
about what GraphQL is.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:49.380
So now could you
tell us a little bit

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.140
about what Apollo
does with GraphQL

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.090
and what services you offer?

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.170
ADAM ZIONTS: Absolutely.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:58.920
Apollo offers kind of the
default implementation

00:01:58.920 --> 00:01:59.640
of GraphQL.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:03.960
So we have an open source
client, an open source server,

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.300
a number of tools
besides those that all

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.120
help people get
started with GraphQL

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.590
and scale it up to
production as well.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:16.020
So starting to write out web
applications and APIs, move

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.210
data from REST, or
even just start out

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:22.260
new projects in GraphQL to
prove out the value of it.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.540
Beyond those kind
of table stakes

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.600
of how to even get started
with GraphQL and build,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.370
we've invested a lot
over the past couple

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.250
of years in the idea of
Apollo Federation, which

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:36.540
allows distinct teams to run
their own GraphQL services

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.950
but then join them together to
provide a single, unified graph

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:42.970
of all of their data.

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:44.880
And so that's also
completely free

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.080
open source, Apollo Federation
that helps big businesses scale

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.060
GraphQL within multiple teams.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.930
And then we have a SaaS
application that helps you--

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:58.800
also has a free version up
to paid and enterprise--

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.620
that helps you understand how
your GraphQL schema is evolving

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:06.090
over time, see who is
using your graph and how,

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.740
and help you to
kind of automate out

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.110
that build process and
keep those teams in sync

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:13.110
without needing to have a lot
of Slack communication or email

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:16.260
communication that generally
happens between backend

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.920
and frontend in order to get
that kind of API work done.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.170
KASLIN FIELDS: So GraphQL
is an open source API.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.200
And Apollo offers
clients and servers,

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.260
which are very popular for
use both with open source

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.550
and in businesses.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:32.310
And then you offer all
of these additional tools

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.680
to help make using
GraphQL at scale

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.770
in large businesses a
lot easier and kind of

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:39.930
make a lot more sense
in the enterprise space,

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:43.410
like DevTools, query
monitoring, this kind of--

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:46.177
I don't know how to
describe it, but like a kind

00:03:46.177 --> 00:03:48.510
of an auditing tool, it sounds
like you were describing?

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:49.510
ADAM ZIONTS: Yeah, yeah.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:53.490
So our mission around
GraphQL is to make

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:56.010
sure that we provide
all of the tools

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:59.910
that businesses need to be able
to scale and manage their graph

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.132
in production safely.

00:04:02.132 --> 00:04:03.090
KASLIN FIELDS: Awesome.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:06.150
So Apollo provides a lot
of tools and services

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.250
to help developers at
these big companies,

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:12.330
and open source as well,
work with data via GraphQL.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:15.660
And you're serving a lot
of users and companies.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:19.376
How does a user typically start
using Apollo's capabilities?

00:04:19.376 --> 00:04:20.769
ADAM ZIONTS: Great question.

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:23.940
Typically, users get
started, or companies

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:27.660
get started, using GraphQL
and using Apollo bit by bit.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.500
We very much believe that
the idea of re-architecting

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:34.560
and starting over from
step one via some big

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.320
like Flag Day and
years of development

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:41.190
is not something that anyone
is trying to do in 2022.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.003
And we highly
recommend that when

00:04:43.003 --> 00:04:44.670
people want to get
started with GraphQL,

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.295
people want to get
started with Apollo,

00:04:46.295 --> 00:04:49.530
they do that by one
feature at a time,

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:52.530
one application at a
time, even one page,

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:55.555
one component at a time when
you're talking about React.

00:04:55.555 --> 00:04:57.180
And so generally,
that's how people get

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:59.610
started, as this
test case, to say

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:03.180
can we take this maybe
complicated feature

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:06.142
and move it over to a graph?

00:05:06.142 --> 00:05:07.940
And then once there's
success in that,

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.770
you can incrementally,
iteratively

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:12.907
start adding more and
more into that graph.

00:05:12.907 --> 00:05:14.990
KASLIN FIELDS: This sounds
like a lot of use cases

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:17.720
that I see in the Cloud
space, especially.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.330
Whenever a business is adopting
any kind of new technology,

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:23.900
it's always best to start with
maybe one or two applications

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:26.870
that you can start shifting
over to that new technology,

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:29.660
and then shift more and more
as you see the benefits,

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:33.230
and as it makes sense, and
as your engineers develop

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:34.715
the skills to use
those tools, too.

00:05:34.715 --> 00:05:35.715
ADAM ZIONTS: Definitely.

00:05:35.715 --> 00:05:38.540
And it's very similar
to Kubernetes,

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:41.000
even, and very much where
that idea of Apollo Federation

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:41.810
comes from--

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:43.946
the idea of being able
to move incrementally,

00:05:43.946 --> 00:05:46.550
to be able to segment
things off separately,

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:48.650
but then bring them
together in the end.

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:52.400
All of those things are
very prominent in the space

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.830
of how to do technology
right, given all the things

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:58.670
that we've learned, and very
much the direction that we

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:00.292
try to come out as Apollo.

00:06:00.292 --> 00:06:01.250
KASLIN FIELDS: Awesome.

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:05.150
So a lot of businesses will
start maybe shifting something

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:08.810
over into GraphQL, start
looking at Apollo services,

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:11.450
and maybe start
moving something over.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:13.640
How does all of this
work on Apollo's side?

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:15.950
And where does
Google Cloud fit in?

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:19.700
ADAM ZIONTS: Yeah, so our
SaaS application that I talked

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:22.910
about before, that helps
track which schemas

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:24.830
are being deployed
over which time,

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:27.890
that helps you to craft
out excellent queries

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:30.590
and mutations, and share
them with your friends

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:33.950
and colleagues, that gives
you insights into who

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:35.510
is using your graph how.

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:39.770
All of that data is hosted
inside of Google Cloud.

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:41.210
And the services
behind the scenes

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:42.680
that help make all
of that possible

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:45.720
is coming from
Google Cloud as well.

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:49.190
We very much believe that our
core competency as a business

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:51.530
should be building
excellent GraphQL

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:55.430
tools that help developers
build excellent apps,

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:57.710
and not learning how
to manage and run

00:06:57.710 --> 00:06:58.835
complicated infrastructure.

00:06:58.835 --> 00:07:00.752
KASLIN FIELDS: So let's
talk a little bit more

00:07:00.752 --> 00:07:01.970
about this architecture.

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:04.790
What are some of the pieces that
go into it, both Google Cloud

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:06.920
products and other
types of technologies

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:08.400
that you use to do what you do?

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:09.150
ADAM ZIONTS: Sure.

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:12.740
So we're running most of this
atop GKE, Google Kubernetes

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:13.430
Engine.

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:17.480
And on top of that, we're using
a number of different databases

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:19.888
and stories behind
the scenes, some

00:07:19.888 --> 00:07:21.680
of the things that
we're running ourselves,

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:26.600
for instance Memcached, Kafka,
and an old data source database

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:30.320
called Druid that helps us
manage that time series data.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.710
A lot of things, we also use
Google Cloud for hosting.

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:36.278
For instance, Cloud SQL,
which are using Postgres,

00:07:36.278 --> 00:07:40.640
ThinkTable, BigQuery,
Pub/Sub, Spanner

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:44.610
is another really big one
that we rely on as well.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:47.660
We also have a couple of
services deployed in AWS

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:50.060
as well, so that we can
have multi-Cloud support

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:54.140
for the most critical
aspects of our system.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:57.712
And I can go into more detail
on any of those things, again.

00:07:57.712 --> 00:08:00.170
KASLIN FIELDS: I think there's
a lot of folks out there who

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:03.830
can relate to a lot of those
components of architecture--

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:07.357
hybrid Cloud, using Kubernetes
is, of course, very popular.

00:08:07.357 --> 00:08:08.690
And I'm a Kubernetes specialist.

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:11.550
So of course, I point that out.

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:14.180
But one thing in there that
I found really interesting

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:16.010
is that you're running
your own Kafka.

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:18.650
I hear Kafka in a lot of
architectures these days.

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:20.480
So could you talk a
little bit about why

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:22.640
you decided to run
Kafka yourselves,

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:24.380
and how that's
been going for you?

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:25.130
ADAM ZIONTS: Yeah.

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:29.990
So I laugh because it's like,
I wouldn't say a sore subject.

00:08:29.990 --> 00:08:31.610
But I certainly
have battle scars.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:33.799
We've been running Kafka
over the last few years.

00:08:33.799 --> 00:08:36.414
And originally, when we
chose to run our own Kafka,

00:08:36.414 --> 00:08:38.539
it was because there just
wasn't an offering for it

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:39.650
on Google Cloud.

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:45.170
There wasn't an offering with
GCP via Confluent Cloud either.

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:47.030
So we started running
our own Kafka then.

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:49.010
And we have definitely--

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:50.658
I think we've
reached a point now

00:08:50.658 --> 00:08:51.950
where things are pretty stable.

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:53.690
We're not-- like,
knock on wood--

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:56.442
but we're not having any
kinds of crazy outages

00:08:56.442 --> 00:08:58.400
or disturbances because
we're running on Kafka.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:00.320
But we had to learn
a lot of things.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:02.540
I remember a lot of
sleepless nights,

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:05.030
just banging our heads against
the proverbial wall trying

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:07.370
to figure out what
the heck was going on.

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:09.050
In particular, there
was this one night

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:11.390
where I had to
escalate to the CTO

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:14.180
at the time, who
was on sabbatical,

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:17.660
because these super
blocks inside of the disk

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:21.860
was actually being completely
corrupted because we were going

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:24.920
over 16 terabytes, which
apparently you can't

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:26.940
do on XT four-file system.

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:28.412
Meanwhile, I'm like--

00:09:28.412 --> 00:09:29.870
I've been using
Kafka a few months.

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:31.203
I had no idea what was going on.

00:09:31.203 --> 00:09:34.610
I felt like I learned much more
in that one night about Kafka

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:39.000
and about disks that I
had in the previous month.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:41.120
But that being said, not
something I particularly

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:42.080
want to repeat.

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.090
So the thing we're actually
looking at right now

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:48.152
is how do we migrate to a Cloud
provider to make sure that now

00:09:48.152 --> 00:09:50.610
that the world is kind of caught
up with our usage of Kafka

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:52.693
and there's plenty of
options out there, make sure

00:09:52.693 --> 00:09:55.370
that as we grow out
the size of Apollo,

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:57.680
we're not going to have
to have a Kafka team just

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:59.652
to manage that queue.

00:09:59.652 --> 00:10:01.860
KASLIN FIELDS: Yeah, that
makes a lot of sense to me.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:03.360
And I think a lot
of folks out there

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:05.990
can relate to having some
part of our infrastructure

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:08.600
that's just, like, we
have to run it ourselves

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:09.980
because reasons.

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:12.110
But it causes a lot of pain.

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:14.570
Maybe eventually, there
are better options.

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:17.300
And you start
considering moving over

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.340
to one of those newer
options so that you

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:21.200
can focus more on
your core business,

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:23.990
rather than these pieces
that you have to run

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:25.220
to make the business work.

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:25.730
ADAM ZIONTS: That's right.

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:27.410
And one thing that
we've also started

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:30.980
to do over the last year or
so has been just investing

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:32.480
more and more in Pub/Sub.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:35.510
So we haven't added a
lot into our Kafka queue.

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:40.310
That's mostly just
quote-unquote "legacy stuff."

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:42.740
And we're starting to think
about that migration now

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:45.110
for the things that are
in Kafka since they're not

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:47.427
super compatible with
Pub/Sub at the moment.

00:10:47.427 --> 00:10:48.260
KASLIN FIELDS: Cool.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:50.270
So one other thing
I want to point out

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:52.400
about your
architecture is when we

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:54.860
were talking about
all of this, I

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:59.900
found out that you are running
pretty much your whole business

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:03.230
on a single Google
Kubernetes Engine cluster.

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:04.640
And as a Kubernetes
specialist, I

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:06.560
found this very
interesting and exciting.

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:09.800
I'd love to hear more about
how that's going for you,

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:13.160
and some of the tools
and kind of tactics

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:17.210
that you use to make a single
cluster for multiple teams,

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:20.330
multiple products
even, work for you?

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:21.410
ADAM ZIONTS: Yeah.

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:24.560
Another great question,
and probably something that

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:26.030
will evolve as we grow.

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:29.240
Like, all of our
Kubernetes Engine

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:31.280
and the way that we
built our backend

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:33.170
was originally out
of one team, as I'm

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:35.778
sure it is for a lot
of folks who have seen

00:11:35.778 --> 00:11:37.070
huge growth in their companies.

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:39.402
And now we have almost a
dozen engineering teams.

00:11:39.402 --> 00:11:40.860
We're about to
begin doubling that.

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:42.443
So I'm sure that a
lot of these things

00:11:42.443 --> 00:11:45.530
are going to change as we
scale and grow out Apollo.

00:11:45.530 --> 00:11:47.840
But ultimately,
being able to have

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:51.560
all of this in one GKE cluster
has been pretty advantageous.

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:55.460
It means that we can wrap all
of our [? kubectl ?] tooling

00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:56.930
to all work in one way.

00:11:56.930 --> 00:11:59.450
It means that whenever
you're inside of the cluster,

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:03.170
you can access logs from
multiple different services

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:05.215
or do operations on
multiple different services

00:12:05.215 --> 00:12:07.340
without needing to log in
and log out, and be like,

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:10.310
am I logged into the right one?

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:12.950
A couple of things that
we have done to make sure

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:14.600
that we don't have
big problems there

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:18.440
is make sure we have separate
namespaces to match production

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:19.520
and staging traffic.

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:22.610
And just make sure to
keep those separate.

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:24.980
And also, just having
different node pools.

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:26.880
Because not every service
has made the same.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:29.600
So we have a bunch of
different node pools

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:32.930
where we can use taints,
and tolerations, and just

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:36.500
selectors to ensure that
those services can be assigned

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:38.180
to the correct node pools.

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:41.520
Or in cases like Kafka,
that's just on its own.

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:43.190
You don't have a
noisy neighbor problem

00:12:43.190 --> 00:12:46.580
where Kafka and some other
thing are all on the same pod

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:48.440
and one is being
starved of resources.

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:49.857
KASLIN FIELDS: I
want to point out

00:12:49.857 --> 00:12:51.830
for anyone that's not
familiar with GKE,

00:12:51.830 --> 00:12:53.510
we have this concept
of node pools,

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:56.600
where a single node pool
is all virtual machine

00:12:56.600 --> 00:12:59.090
instances of the same type,
the same kind of hardware

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:01.170
configuration, the
same pieces there.

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:03.800
So then you can use Kubernetes
taints and tolerations

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:06.265
to manage where your
workloads are run,

00:13:06.265 --> 00:13:08.390
making sure that they're
run on the right hardware,

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:10.097
the right machines
for that workload

00:13:10.097 --> 00:13:11.180
ADAM ZIONTS: That's right.

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:13.250
And that's a--

00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:15.385
I say it's all pretty much good.

00:13:15.385 --> 00:13:16.760
Kind of a similar
start to Kafka,

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:18.470
like we have learned our--

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:20.960
we've learned our
lessons the hard way.

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:24.020
For instance, we,
in general, are

00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:27.020
OK with having pods from
staging and from production

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:28.010
on the same nodes.

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:30.250
That's not a huge deal.

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:31.250
Could be a little scary.

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:36.770
For instance, one time
we were using local SSDs

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:38.420
to store a whole bunch of state.

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:40.820
And they were just picked
up by the same file path.

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:44.370
And we ended up leaking data
between staging and production,

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:46.430
which had this massive
effect on wiping out

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:47.760
a swath of our database.

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:50.210
This was years ago.

00:13:50.210 --> 00:13:52.580
And we didn't affect any
customer traffic because of it.

00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:56.490
But it was the most perplexing
issue you could imagine.

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:58.700
And so, definitely,
there is a huge advantage

00:13:58.700 --> 00:14:00.590
in keeping things as
separate as possible.

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:03.890
And as I mentioned before, as
we grow out more and more teams,

00:14:03.890 --> 00:14:06.680
our goal and the way that we
think about team management

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:08.450
at Apollo, is that
each team should

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:12.320
be able to its own stack of
architecture, top to bottom,

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:14.820
as much as possible, and
do that independently.

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:18.800
So that might mean having a
different namespace or even

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:21.005
entire different
cluster per team,

00:14:21.005 --> 00:14:22.880
and being able to enjoy
those things together

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:24.742
across some kind
of [? period. ?]

00:14:24.742 --> 00:14:25.700
KASLIN FIELDS: Awesome.

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:28.310
I think as you grow, like
you said, a lot of folks

00:14:28.310 --> 00:14:30.290
start out with
technologies that work

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:32.510
when you're one team that's
trying to do a thing.

00:14:32.510 --> 00:14:35.090
And then as you start
growing more teams,

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:37.580
there are all these types of
challenges that you encounter.

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:39.830
And so you have to start
thinking about different ways

00:14:39.830 --> 00:14:41.402
to do things.

00:14:41.402 --> 00:14:42.610
So that makes a lot of sense.

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:45.380
ADAM ZIONTS: Yeah, and for me,
that's part of the fun of this.

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:47.670
I remember starting
off in engineering,

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:51.450
I had this idea of wanting
to do everything perfectly.

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:54.227
And as you get in, months
in, you're like, OK,

00:14:54.227 --> 00:14:55.310
it's all about trade-offs.

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:57.350
You're not going to do
the perfect thing ever.

00:14:57.350 --> 00:14:59.330
You're going to do the right
thing for the right time.

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:00.470
And six months from
now, that's not going

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:01.760
to be the right thing anymore.

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:03.260
Nothing lasts.

00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:05.010
And I don't know,
I find that problem

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:08.123
really fun and
interesting, of balancing

00:15:08.123 --> 00:15:09.540
all of those
different priorities,

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:13.860
and making good decisions,
and then fixing the airplane

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:16.497
as you're in the
air, so to speak.

00:15:16.497 --> 00:15:17.455
KASLIN FIELDS: Awesome.

00:15:17.455 --> 00:15:19.950
So Apollo has a lot of
innovation coming up,

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:20.820
it sounds like.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:22.410
As you grow up
more teams, you're

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:24.243
going to consider
different ways that you're

00:15:24.243 --> 00:15:26.580
going to do your
Kubernetes architecture.

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:30.210
We talked about Kafka possibly
moving into a managed service.

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:32.910
Anything else you want to point
out about ways that Apollo

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:35.070
is kind of innovating,
both on their business

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:37.020
and on their infrastructure
architecture?

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:38.520
ADAM ZIONTS: Yeah.

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:40.090
Those are all really
good examples.

00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:43.650
I think that, in general,
as you grow and scale

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:46.590
out the number of teams that
you have, there is a lot-- like,

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:47.970
a lot of people
talk about scale.

00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:49.800
And the first thing
that usually comes

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:53.317
to mind for me is like,
what is the TPS that you're

00:15:53.317 --> 00:15:54.150
trying to deal with?

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:56.160
How much more traffic
are you getting?

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:59.430
But as I've kind of
gone through my career,

00:15:59.430 --> 00:16:02.190
I've started to think much more
of scale in terms of people.

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:04.650
How many people can
you have actively

00:16:04.650 --> 00:16:07.980
collaborating on this code
base, on this infrastructure?

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:10.018
And a ton of work
is going to be done

00:16:10.018 --> 00:16:12.060
over the next several--
over the next six months,

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:14.497
over the next year,
probably ad infinitum,

00:16:14.497 --> 00:16:16.080
to make that more
and more achievable,

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:18.163
like we were talking about
with trade-offs before.

00:16:18.163 --> 00:16:20.670
So that means separating
this infrastructure

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:23.440
so that each team can
its own architecture.

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:25.710
That means making it easier
to deploy things and have

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:27.930
the observability that
you want out of the box.

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:30.540
That means starting to
use more managed services

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:34.140
instead of hosting
things ourselves.

00:16:34.140 --> 00:16:36.510
We're also dealing with
that other side of scale,

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:39.600
where we have customers
like Walmart, and Netflix,

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:43.657
and Airbnb, on down the
line, more and more,

00:16:43.657 --> 00:16:45.240
who are starting to
adopt GraphQL, who

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:46.980
are starting to adopt Apollo.

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:50.820
And being able to lean
in a little bit more

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:54.240
to more events-driven
systems, making sure

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:57.990
that we have partial degradation
rather than complete failures

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:00.210
in places, starting to do
more chaos engineering.

00:17:00.210 --> 00:17:02.940
There's a lot of fun
things ahead of us

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:04.800
infrastructure-wise,
to make sure

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:07.950
that we can hit both of
those kinds of scale,

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:11.190
both the scale that comes from
just the raw growth of traffic

00:17:11.190 --> 00:17:13.740
as well as the engineers
that we're all collaborating

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:15.142
with now.

00:17:15.142 --> 00:17:16.200
KASLIN FIELDS: Awesome.

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:18.430
And speaking of
scale and growth,

00:17:18.430 --> 00:17:20.910
